                    Design: I removed the extra `unsqueeze`                     
                    on mixup labels so that the target                          
                    shape `(batch, 1)` matches the output                       
                    shape, avoiding the `(batch, 1, 1)`                         
                    mismatch. The loss is now computed                          
                    directly with `labels_a` and                                
                    `labels_b`. Everything else remains                         
                    same, and we print the validation AUROC                     
                    at the end and save `submission.csv` in                     
                    `working/`.                                                 
                    Results: The training script ran                            
                    successfully without errors. The model                      
                    was trained for 5 epochs using mixup                        
                    augmentation and evaluated with                             
                    test-time augmentation (TTA) on the                         
                    validation set. The final validation                        
                    AUROC achieved was 0.8935, indicating                       
                    good performance in classifying                             
                    malignant versus benign skin lesions.                       
                    The script also includes a prediction                       
                    function that applies TTA and saves                         
                    predictions to a CSV file.                                  
                    Validation Metric: 0.8935                                   
                                                                                
                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: I will replace the Adam                             
                    optimizer with AdamW including a small                      
                    weight decay and add a                                      
                    CosineAnnealingLR scheduler over the 5                      
                    epochs to gradually decrease the                            
                    learning rate, improving both                               
                    convergence and generalization. This                        
                    lightweight change doesn't affect the                       
                    data pipeline or model architecture and                     
                    often yields a higher AUROC with                            
                    minimal extra code. The weight decay of                     
                    1e-4 helps regularize the training. The                     
                    rest of the pipeline, including                             
                    augmentations and TTA for validation,                       
                    remains unchanged. The script prints                        
                    the final validation AUROC and saves                        
                    the trained model and a `predict()`                         
                    function for inference.                                     
