                    a competition. You are provided with a                      
                    previously developed solution below and                     
                    should improve it in order to further                       
                    increase the (test time) performance.                       
                    For this you should first outline a                         
                    brief plan in natural language for how                      
                    the solution can be improved and then                       
                    implement this improvement in Python                        
                    based on the provided previous                              
                    solution.                                                   
                                                                                
                    # Task description                                          
                                                                                
                    ## Task goal                                                
                                                                                
                    I'm a dermatologist. My colleagues and                      
                    I have curated a skin lesion dataset                        
                    over the past several years. I need a                       
                    model trained on this data to classify                      
                    lesions as malignant or benign. The                         
                    trained model should be saved, and I                        
                    need a function that takes a folder                         
                    path of new images and returns a                            
                    malignancy probability (0–1) for each                       
                    image.                                                      
                                                                                
                    ## Task evaluation                                          
                                                                                
                    Area under the receiver operator curve                      
                    (AUROC)                                                     
                                                                                
                    # Memory                                                    
                                                                                
                    Design: We fine-tune a pretrained                           
                    ResNet18 on the binary task of                              
                    'malignant' versus all other labels,                        
                    using simple augmentations and a                            
                    hold-out validation split. We load                          
                    image paths and labels from the CSV                         
                    into a custom PyTorch Dataset, train                        
                    for a few epochs on GPU if available,                       
                    and evaluate using AUROC on the                             
                    validation set. Finally, we provide a                       
                    predict() function that takes a folder                      
                    of new images and returns malignancy                        
                    probabilities. The script prints the                        
                    validation AUROC at the end.                                
                    Results: The training script executed                       
                    successfully without any errors. The                        
                    model was trained for 3 epochs on the                       
                    skin lesion dataset, achieving a strong                     
                    validation AUROC of 0.9041, indicating                      
                    good discriminative performance for                         
                    malignancy classification. The provided                     
                    prediction function correctly processes                     
                    new images and outputs malignancy                           
                    probabilities.                                              
                    Validation Metric: 0.9041                                   
                                                                                
                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: We will fine-tune a pretrained                      
                    EfficientNet-B0 from the timm library                       
                    on our binary malignant vs                                  
                    benign+non-neoplastic task using 5-fold                     
                    stratified cross-validation. Simple                         
                    on-the-fly augmentations (random crop,                      
                    flip, rotation) will be applied during                      
                    training, with standardized resizing at                     
                    validation. We will train each fold for                     
                    3 epochs with BCEWithLogitsLoss and                         
                    compute AUROC on out-of-fold                                
                    predictions, printing the mean CV                           
                    AUROC. Finally, a predict() function                        
                    will load any folder of images, apply                       
                    the same preprocessing, and output                          
                    malignancy probabilities.                                   
                    Results: The training script ran                            
                    successfully without any errors. The                        
                    model was trained using 5-fold                              
                    cross-validation on the skin lesion                         
                    dataset, achieving a mean AUROC of                          
                    0.8718, which indicates good                                
                    discriminative performance for                              
                    malignancy classification. The final                        
                    model was trained on the full dataset                       
                    and saved correctly for later                               
                    inference.                                                  
                    Validation Metric: 0.8718                                   
                                                                                
                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: We fine-tune a pretrained                           
                    DenseNet121 on the binary task of                           
                    distinguishing malignant versus                             
                    non-malignant lesions, using an 80/20                       
                    stratified train/validation split.                          
                    Training includes random resized crops,                     
                    horizontal flips, and rotations, while                      
                    validation uses center-cropped                              
                    resizing, and we optimize with Adam and                     
                    BCEWithLogitsLoss over 5 epochs. After                      
                    training, we compute and print the                          
                    AUROC on the validation set. The model                      
                    is saved to disk, and a reusable                            
                    predict() function loads the model,                         
                    processes a folder of new images, and                       
                    outputs malignancy probabilities,                           
                    saving them to                                              
                    `working/submission.csv`.                                   
                    Results: The training script ran                            
                    successfully without any errors or                          
                    bugs. The model was trained for 5                           
                    epochs on the skin lesion dataset using                     
                    a DenseNet121 architecture with                             
                    appropriate data augmentations and                          
                    normalization. The validation AUROC                         
                    achieved was 0.9136, indicating strong                      
                    classification performance for                              
                    distinguishing malignant from benign                        
                    lesions. The prediction function is                         
                    implemented to load the trained model                       
                    and output malignancy probabilities for                     
                    new images, saving results to a CSV                         
                    file.                                                       
                    Validation Metric: 0.9136                                   
                                                                                
                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: We fine-tune a pretrained                           
                    MobileNetV2 on malignant vs                                 
                    non-malignant lesions using an 80/20                        
                    stratified split. Training employs                          
                    random resized crops, horizontal flips,                     
                    and color jitter, while validation uses                     
                    center-cropped resizing. The classifier                     
                    head is replaced to output a single                         
                    logit and trained with AdamW and a                          
                    cosine annealing LR scheduler for 4                         
                    epochs. We evaluate the final model on                      
                    the validation set using AUROC and                          
                    print the result. A reusable                                
                    `predict()` function is provided to                         
                    load the trained model and return                           
                    malignancy probabilities for new images                     
                    in a given folder.                                          
                    Results: The training script executed                       
                    successfully without errors. The model                      
                    trained for 4 epochs with steadily                          
                    decreasing training loss, indicating                        
                    effective learning. The validation                          
                    AUROC of 0.9023 demonstrates strong                         
                    discriminatory performance of the model                     
                    in classifying malignant vs. benign                         
                    skin lesions. The saved model and the                       
                    provided prediction function should be                      
                    reliable for inference on new images.                       
                    Validation Metric: 0.9023                                   
                                                                                
                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: I propose fine-tuning a                             
                    pretrained ConvNeXt-Tiny model from the                     
                    timm library on the binary task of                          
                    malignant versus non-malignant lesions                      
                    using an 80/20 stratified hold-out                          
                    split. Images are loaded from disk with                     
                    simple augmentations (random resized                        
                    crop, horizontal flip, color jitter)                        
                    during training and                                         
                    resized+center-cropped for validation.                      
                    The model head is adapted to a single                       
                    logit and trained with                                      
                    BCEWithLogitsLoss and AdamW over 4                          
                    epochs. We compute and print the                            
                    validation AUROC and provide a                              
                    `predict()` function that loads a saved                     
                    model to output malignancy                                  
                    probabilities for new images.                               
                    Results: The training script ran                            
                    successfully without errors. The model                      
                    was trained for 4 epochs on the skin                        
                    lesion dataset with a ConvNeXt Tiny                         
                    architecture pretrained on ImageNet.                        
                    Training loss decreased steadily each                       
                    epoch, indicating effective learning.                       
                    The validation AUROC of approximately                       
                    0.8997 demonstrates strong                                  
                    classification performance for                              
                    distinguishing malignant from benign                        
                    lesions. The model was saved correctly                      
                    and a prediction function was provided                      
                    for inference on new images.                                
                    Validation Metric: 0.8997                                   
                                                                                
